          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "The guidance defines an uncomplicated urinary tract infection as acute cystitis (bladder infection) in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. Anything outside that definition, such as pregnancy, being a man, a catheter, a structural problem, stones, a weakened immune system, or suspected kidney involvement, falls under a separate \"complicated\" pathway. (Source: *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*, infectious diseases and urology society educational summary.)\n\nFor diagnosis, the guidance says that when someone has typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, empiric treatment (treatment without a lab culture) is appropriate. A urine dipstick adds little in this group: a positive result confirms what was already likely, and a negative result does not reliably rule infection out. The dipstick is more useful when symptoms are unclear. (Same document.)\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover which treatments are chosen, how long they last, when a urine culture is needed, or how recurrence is managed, even though the document's title mentions these topics. For those questions, a clinician or pharmacist is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
